WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.429 --> 00:00:06.609
The title of today's webinar is Using BLAST
Well.

00:00:06.609 --> 00:00:12.120
I'm please to present Tom Madden who is the
lead of our BLAST team here at NCBI.

00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:15.580
I'm going to turn it over to Tom Madden now.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:17.539
Thank you Peter.

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:23.270
This is Tom Madden, and let me give a little
bit of background on this webinar.

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:30.230
This was originally written this up as a talk
for a Hackathon we had at the NIH about a

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:32.719
month ago.

00:00:32.719 --> 00:00:34.510
It was well received, and then we had other
ideas.

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:38.329
We modified this a bit and we're using the
webinar to share those ideas.

00:00:38.329 --> 00:00:45.250
Since it was for a Hackathon it was centered
around and targeted to standalone BLAST-plus

00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:53.469
users as opposed to people who come to the
web page to run BLAST.

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:58.090
If you have never run standalone BLAST let
me first say what that is.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:04.030
basically that means you download our executables
from the FTP site and then you go to a command

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:06.400
line and type something to run them.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.960
I will have examples later on.

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:14.990
If you have never done that you may still
find this interesting because I will talk

00:01:14.990 --> 00:01:22.420
about some of the BLAST databases and what
they contain.

00:01:22.420 --> 00:01:23.530
Let me get going.

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:26.770
Hopefully you will find this interesting.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.920
This is the first what I call the boilerplate
slide.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:33.400
What is blast.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:39.020
Most people are familiar with the basics of
what BLAST is supposed to do.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:44.640
The main idea is that it calculates similarity
for biological sequences, and you get your

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:50.579
query and scan a database and look for similar
sequences.

00:01:50.579 --> 00:01:55.770
You can decide whether or not those similar
sequences are homologous.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:02:02.680
It's pretty fast, and it uses statistical
theory to help you determine whether it occurred

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.869
by chance.

00:02:04.869 --> 00:02:10.289
So, first I'm going to talk about the different
databases that we have.

00:02:10.289 --> 00:02:15.909
Our database descriptions can actually be
kind of confusing, this is something that

00:02:15.909 --> 00:02:21.349
we need to work on in as maybe doing a better
job of this.

00:02:21.349 --> 00:02:25.169
There are two main databases that people often
use.

00:02:25.169 --> 00:02:31.669
One is the non-redundant protein database,
called NR, which is the largest collection

00:02:31.669 --> 00:02:33.709
of proteins.

00:02:33.709 --> 00:02:39.739
It gives you proteins from GenBank CDSs and
PDB and SwissProt, which will give you everything

00:02:39.739 --> 00:02:45.069
though it excludes proteins from environmental
samples from WGS and it also excludes patents

00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:47.989
sequences.

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:50.890
Those are searchable separately in separate
databases.

00:02:50.890 --> 00:02:56.139
And the other database that a lot of people
search is the nucleotide (nt) one.

00:02:56.139 --> 00:03:00.299
And that has a lot of collections nucleotides.

00:03:00.299 --> 00:03:07.810
It does exclude some of these like ESTs, GSS,
WGS, and a whole bunch of other sequences.

00:03:07.810 --> 00:03:13.040
It excludes assembled genomes like the human
genome and mouse genome.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.349
And that is something that you can search
separately.

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:24.340
And it's important to realize what is in these
databases as you go looking at them.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:29.439
It can be confusing.

00:03:29.439 --> 00:03:33.189
One way to look at this is a Venn diagram.

00:03:33.189 --> 00:03:35.040
Pictures worth a 1000 words.

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:39.260
Venn diagram is worth 10,000.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:49.129
What we have on the left here, (Peter can
they see my mouse?)

00:03:49.129 --> 00:03:50.810
I guess you can see my mouse.

00:03:50.810 --> 00:03:59.579
This large circle is NR nonredundant database,
inside of NR, it includes a few other databases

00:03:59.579 --> 00:04:10.369
which is a RefSeq protein which are sequences
that have been annotated here at the NCBI.

00:04:10.369 --> 00:04:13.400
A little bit more validation has been done
on them.

00:04:13.400 --> 00:04:18.729
Also included is Swissprot sequences, if you
are familiar with SwissProt that is a smaller

00:04:18.729 --> 00:04:21.639
database which are also very well curated.

00:04:21.639 --> 00:04:30.560
Inside the RefSeq protein is the landmark
database, which we use for SmartBLAST, and

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:38.871
that is a set from proteomes from 26 different
organisms, which gives you a nonredundant

00:04:38.871 --> 00:04:43.140
spread out set.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:52.260
On top of that is PDB, those are sequences
associated with PDB structures.

00:04:52.260 --> 00:04:56.840
The size [of the Venn diagram circles] is
not really to scale.

00:04:56.840 --> 00:05:00.009
More to give you an idea of what things are.

00:05:00.009 --> 00:05:03.830
RefSeq is about half of NR, so it is very
large.

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.360
I just threw this here [SwissProt circle].

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:12.820
A lot of the sequences in Swissprot do overlap
with RefSeq, and maybe they overlap 100%,

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:14.479
I'm not sure.

00:05:14.479 --> 00:05:19.180
I do know that landmark is completely enclosed
with RefSeq.

00:05:19.180 --> 00:05:26.740
The other independent databases: there are
Metagenomic proteins.

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:31.659
And basically a lot of these come from WGS,
the transcriptome shotgun assembly proteins

00:05:31.659 --> 00:05:35.790
and patents.

00:05:35.790 --> 00:05:45.240
Before I move on, let's say, NR has 171,000,000
sequences and 63,000,000,000 residues.

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:49.300
it is pretty large.

00:05:49.300 --> 00:06:02.749
RefSeq is 118 million sequences and 45,000,000,000
residues so RefSeq is a substantial fraction

00:06:02.749 --> 00:06:04.669
of NR.

00:06:04.669 --> 00:06:13.100
Swissprot has 470,000 sequences and 178,000,000
residues.

00:06:13.100 --> 00:06:18.810
Landmark as I show you here is very small.

00:06:18.810 --> 00:06:23.490
One question is why do I search these and
you can think of this like a use case.

00:06:23.490 --> 00:06:33.830
Why would people want to run a search against
NR, as opposed to RefSeq protein or landmark.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:38.070
The way to think about NR, the nonredundant
one, is it's almost all of the proteins at

00:06:38.070 --> 00:06:39.170
the NCBI.

00:06:39.170 --> 00:06:44.160
You might be looking for homologs and other
organisms, you want to look for protein in

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:45.530
other organisms.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:48.150
Or maybe you just want to identify your protein.

00:06:48.150 --> 00:06:53.770
I don't know what my protein is and I'll search
it against the NCBI.

00:06:53.770 --> 00:07:01.350
RefSeq protein is basically the same task,
you have well curated proteins in RefSeq,

00:07:01.350 --> 00:07:07.550
and there you might search for homologs in
other organisms and you might just identify

00:07:07.550 --> 00:07:08.550
a protein.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:15.720
On the other hand, Landmark, has got only
26, or 23, different organisms spread across

00:07:15.720 --> 00:07:20.419
a great taxonomic range.

00:07:20.419 --> 00:07:26.889
That will allow you to find homologs in distally-related
model organisms that represents a wide range

00:07:26.889 --> 00:07:29.159
of taxonomic groups.

00:07:29.159 --> 00:07:35.229
These are not necessarily all of the use cases
you can have for a database, and if you're

00:07:35.229 --> 00:07:38.939
not doing this, it doesn't mean that you are
doing something wrong.

00:07:38.939 --> 00:07:45.052
If you would like to share in the [question]
box what you are doing with the protein or

00:07:45.052 --> 00:07:50.199
nucleotide databases, and we have time to
go through those, and that might be useful

00:07:50.199 --> 00:07:55.080
for people to hear how these databases are
being used.

00:07:55.080 --> 00:07:57.300
It might be interesting for me too.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:02.690
[The slide] says at the bottom it is not an
exhaustive list.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:11.240
Moving on to the nucleotide databases, it's
a lot more confusing in a way.

00:08:11.240 --> 00:08:16.610
Now here you have the nucleotide, I talked
about earlier.

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:21.430
This is 48 million sequences and 200 billion
bases.

00:08:21.430 --> 00:08:24.460
This is substantial.

00:08:24.460 --> 00:08:31.430
Inside of it is the RefSeq RNA, and that's
23 million sequence and 57 billion bases,

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:37.080
about a third of the NT.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:46.389
Then we have the smaller 16S ribosomal sequences,
I will come back to tell you what these are

00:08:46.389 --> 00:08:56.899
for, but the 16S is well curated sequences
that you can actually use, to verify the taxonomy

00:08:56.899 --> 00:08:58.920
for your sequences.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.589
We have this group on the left.

00:09:01.589 --> 00:09:05.540
And then over here [on the right side of the
slide] we have this massive set which is RefSeq

00:09:05.540 --> 00:09:13.940
genomes, which basically all of the genomes
that are in the RefSeq at NCBI.

00:09:13.940 --> 00:09:22.220
That's going to include most of the eukaryotic
genomes, Human genomes, mouse genomes, and

00:09:22.220 --> 00:09:32.210
many other genomes, also will have alligator
genome, crocodile genome, so this ends up

00:09:32.210 --> 00:09:33.440
being a very large set.

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:39.041
Within a set of RefSeq genomes, we have what
is called representative genomes, and that

00:09:39.041 --> 00:09:46.050
gives you, rather than giving you all redundant
sets of genomes, it gives you the one best

00:09:46.050 --> 00:09:51.630
genome we think if representative and so that
would mean that rather than having three copies

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:55.540
of this human genome, and there's probably
more than that now, that you just have the

00:09:55.540 --> 00:09:59.730
one in the representative genome set.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:07.829
Outside of this is other things like express
sequence tags, ESTs, patent, again, we've

00:10:07.829 --> 00:10:15.800
separated out the patent again, the rationale
for that is, most scientists are not interested

00:10:15.800 --> 00:10:22.449
in seeing the patent record, they don't have
a lot of scientific information on them.

00:10:22.449 --> 00:10:27.769
If you are really interested in patents, then
you're probably not going to be interested

00:10:27.769 --> 00:10:30.730
in all of these other sequences over here.

00:10:30.730 --> 00:10:40.110
There are other older sets of databases like
high throughput genome sequences, phases O,1,2.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:46.810
These were really popular about 20 years ago
when the human genome was being assembled

00:10:46.810 --> 00:10:54.259
and since then, you don't really get any additions
to HTGS or EST anymore, and to my thinking,

00:10:54.259 --> 00:11:00.000
they aren't that interesting because they
will not have the newest sequence.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:06.750
On top of that we have the BLAST through the
webpage, and through the standalone executables

00:11:06.750 --> 00:11:11.051
offers you the whole genome sequences and
I cannot even tell you how large that is,

00:11:11.051 --> 00:11:18.490
it is very large, as well as SRA, which is
the NCBI sequence read archive.

00:11:18.490 --> 00:11:24.870
So at least I know for SRA they measure this
and talk about petabytes and peta-bases, WGS

00:11:24.870 --> 00:11:29.180
is also not small.

00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:32.839
Again let's look at some of the use cases
for the nucleotide Databases.

00:11:32.839 --> 00:11:37.630
And what we see people doing with NT, this
is the largest database.

00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:42.560
Now maybe they're going to verify the identity
of the DNA, let me, I just sequenced something,

00:11:42.560 --> 00:11:46.630
I think I know what it is and I just want
to verify it real quick.

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:51.790
I might have the case that I really need to
identify the DNA.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:59.899
I've done some sequencing runs, I have a lot
of DNA, and I want to run to see each of these

00:11:59.899 --> 00:12:03.230
sequences are part of.

00:12:03.230 --> 00:12:12.480
So RefSeq RNA as it says here, implies that
these are RNAs, mRNAs and other RNAs, curated,

00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:20.889
provided by the RefSeq group at NCBI, for
this, you can identify unknown mRNA transcripts,

00:12:20.889 --> 00:12:26.910
you could identify coding regions and genomic
sequences, and you could look for other RNA

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:28.589
types of sequence.

00:12:28.589 --> 00:12:37.680
16S Database, that I talked about, this is
very useful because it allows you to classify

00:12:37.680 --> 00:12:44.420
the taxonomy of the samples, and this data
has been maintained at the NCBI, so we are

00:12:44.420 --> 00:12:48.430
pretty sure the taxonomy.

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:51.029
We think this is pretty reliable.

00:12:51.029 --> 00:12:55.459
It also allows you to validate the quality
of the sequences, for example, look for misassembly

00:12:55.459 --> 00:12:58.699
or contaminations or such.

00:12:58.699 --> 00:13:04.540
Finally, there's these other sequences we
talk about, like the human genome.

00:13:04.540 --> 00:13:09.279
The question might be, when should I use NT,
or when should I use the human genome?

00:13:09.279 --> 00:13:14.870
For the human genome case, this is when I
have a gene, and I want to find a specific

00:13:14.870 --> 00:13:19.840
gene on a specific genome, which would be
for blastn or tblastn.

00:13:19.840 --> 00:13:24.800
Blastn, which would take a nucleotide query
and blast it against the human genome.

00:13:24.800 --> 00:13:31.080
Actually, I'm sorry, TBLASTN doesn't apply
here that's wrong.

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:35.870
And the other case is I might map reads to
a new genome, with a new program that we call

00:13:35.870 --> 00:13:39.089
Magic-BLAST, which is splice aware aligner.

00:13:39.089 --> 00:13:42.550
I will mention that later on.

00:13:42.550 --> 00:13:49.320
As I mentioned before, NT is pretty large,
about 200 billion bases, RefSeq RNA is about

00:13:49.320 --> 00:13:57.721
57 billion bases, and the 16S is about 30
million bases, so the other two are 'b's and

00:13:57.721 --> 00:14:01.360
16S is an 'm'.

00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:06.410
And the human genome stays at about 3 billion
bases.

00:14:06.410 --> 00:14:12.339
If we go up here [pointing to the RefSeq genome
circle], just to be clear, this RefSeq genome

00:14:12.339 --> 00:14:17.690
here is 1.2 tera bases.

00:14:17.690 --> 00:14:25.950
I guess that is trillion bases, so that is
really very large.

00:14:25.950 --> 00:14:32.600
Now I want to switch gears and I've gone over
the databases and switch gears and talk about

00:14:32.600 --> 00:14:35.430
BLAST parameters.

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:38.529
This is something that people have found confusing
in the past.

00:14:38.529 --> 00:14:42.480
And will probably find confusing in the future.

00:14:42.480 --> 00:14:45.680
This is why I want to address it.

00:14:45.680 --> 00:14:49.649
There are a large number of blast parameters
and a lot of them have to do with some sort

00:14:49.649 --> 00:14:54.100
of algorithm tuning.

00:14:54.100 --> 00:15:03.069
Most of those you can leave alone and they
have been tuned for a good default.

00:15:03.069 --> 00:15:06.940
So I would not, most of those I would not
change.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:13.500
Also, on the standalone [blast], there is
this 'task' parameter that you can set.

00:15:13.500 --> 00:15:18.570
So by the 'task' parameter, targets your search
to a specific use case.

00:15:18.570 --> 00:15:27.350
For example -task of blastn gives you a word
size of 11, and a pretty sensitive search,

00:15:27.350 --> 00:15:33.759
whereas -task of MegaBLAST, invokes the greedy
megablast alignment and that has an initial

00:15:33.759 --> 00:15:38.699
word size of 28, which means I need an exact
match 28 letters before I would do anything

00:15:38.699 --> 00:15:42.730
and then uses a greedy method of extension.

00:15:42.730 --> 00:15:50.250
Generally speaking, it is faster but less
sensitive but often does the job.

00:15:50.250 --> 00:16:00.880
On blastp, you do a -task of blastp, which
is the standard blastp search that people

00:16:00.880 --> 00:16:07.079
have known about for quite a while, first
coming out in [19]97.

00:16:07.079 --> 00:16:12.649
Then there is also -task blastp-fast, which
is still pretty sensitive but uses a larger

00:16:12.649 --> 00:16:18.529
word size and a compressed alphabet to do
the search actually faster.

00:16:18.529 --> 00:16:26.180
That is actually a good way to approach the
problems by setting the dash[-] task flag,

00:16:26.180 --> 00:16:30.720
and there are other parameters that I would
encourage people look at.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:34.259
For example, there is the expect value.

00:16:34.259 --> 00:16:39.269
If you are not familiar with an expect value,
I will tell you real quick what it is.

00:16:39.269 --> 00:16:43.570
Generally, an expect value, lower is better.

00:16:43.570 --> 00:16:50.060
That means a lower expect value is better,
like a P value, the lower the better it is.

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:57.839
It is, the default, think of a false positive
rate.

00:16:57.839 --> 00:17:03.839
The number of hits equal to or greater than
the score expected to arise by chance.

00:17:03.839 --> 00:17:08.770
If that last sentence is a little long to
remember, just think that lower is better,

00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:11.110
false positive rate.

00:17:11.110 --> 00:17:12.560
The default now is 10.

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:21.310
which is actually a little high for searches,
it says if you have an expect value of 10,

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:27.530
you probably have 10 results in there that
just arose by chance.

00:17:27.530 --> 00:17:37.260
Often you want to lower the expect value to
0.01, or 10 to the -5 or 10 to the minus 30,

00:17:37.260 --> 00:17:42.790
it really depends on what you will do to the
results.

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:47.390
If you want to make sure you get a lot of
results, in that you have to comb through

00:17:47.390 --> 00:17:53.520
stuff, maybe think about what people call
the twilight zone, where it's not really clear

00:17:53.520 --> 00:18:01.450
whether this is a real homolog, or some kind
of artefact, then maybe you want to go to

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:02.620
zero point one or 0.01.

00:18:02.620 --> 00:18:09.230
On the other hand, if you have a pipeline,
and you want to make sure that these are really

00:18:09.230 --> 00:18:16.330
strong matches, and you have hundreds or thousands
of millions of sequences, you probably want

00:18:16.330 --> 00:18:22.390
to have a really low expect value to prevent
you from having false positives.

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:26.760
Okay, so other parameters that you want to
look at.

00:18:26.760 --> 00:18:32.520
Things like word size, and I had mentioned
this before in the task [parameter], BLASTn

00:18:32.520 --> 00:18:36.190
default is 11.

00:18:36.190 --> 00:18:40.930
That means that you need 11 bases that match
exactly.

00:18:40.930 --> 00:18:46.030
Before you do anything between two sequences,
the two sequences need to have 11 bases matching

00:18:46.030 --> 00:18:54.790
exactly, and for MegaBLAST that is 28, which
makes megablast less sensitive but the databases

00:18:54.790 --> 00:19:00.750
as we talked about are actually pretty big
so a megablast search with 28, a lot of mRNAs,

00:19:00.750 --> 00:19:05.820
you'll probably get a fair number of hits
and you may get all the information you may

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:07.080
need.

00:19:07.080 --> 00:19:11.380
If you're running the, getting the standard
report, you can change the number of descriptions,

00:19:11.380 --> 00:19:14.500
as in the number of alignments.

00:19:14.500 --> 00:19:22.860
You can also change the, what we call max_target_seqs,
and the tabular, XML, or JSON [formats].

00:19:22.860 --> 00:19:27.400
I'll be showing you examples of these formats
in a little bit.

00:19:27.400 --> 00:19:28.420
But before I do that.

00:19:28.420 --> 00:19:34.450
I want to go back and talk about something
people have been discussing recently on Twitter

00:19:34.450 --> 00:19:36.840
and other places.

00:19:36.840 --> 00:19:47.040
So I have my search here, right, and I am
running blastn and the default task for blastn,

00:19:47.040 --> 00:19:52.650
this is really a MegaBLAST run, and I'm running
against NT, these 200 billion bases, and I've

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:55.270
got my query, it's part of an E.coli sequence.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:20:00.080
I set a low set value, 10 to the -6.

00:20:00.080 --> 00:20:05.130
I just want to see one match, max_target_seqs
is 1.

00:20:05.130 --> 00:20:12.170
I want to see the first hit that BLAST finds,
this is a way of getting tabular output, so

00:20:12.170 --> 00:20:18.340
I can say '-outfmt', so I want to see the
query accession, the database accession, the

00:20:18.340 --> 00:20:23.780
start and the end of the database sequence,
the expect value of the match, the scientific

00:20:23.780 --> 00:20:27.180
name and title.

00:20:27.180 --> 00:20:30.920
We have some documentation on this and I'll
go through other examples of this 'outfmt'

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:31.920
here.

00:20:31.920 --> 00:20:36.440
But the main thing is, if you run it, you
get one match, you've asked BLAST to give

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:37.440
you one match.

00:20:37.440 --> 00:20:40.930
It gives you that.

00:20:40.930 --> 00:20:46.230
I named this query E.coli, this is the accession
of the database sequence.

00:20:46.230 --> 00:20:50.540
So what we have here.

00:20:50.540 --> 00:20:58.470
This just says the match starts here at 3.9
million [3961744], and then goes on to here

00:20:58.470 --> 00:21:00.640
[3959157].

00:21:00.640 --> 00:21:09.920
It has an expect value of basically zero which
means just below ten to the minus 180.

00:21:09.920 --> 00:21:11.670
Very small.

00:21:11.670 --> 00:21:15.060
A large bit score which means it is high.

00:21:15.060 --> 00:21:22.770
The organism is E. coli, and the title is
isolate NQ3 chromosome complete genome.

00:21:22.770 --> 00:21:24.180
Okay, very good.

00:21:24.180 --> 00:21:25.380
So I put this in here.

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:33.330
I got one E.coli match, this is the only best
result, but let's go back and look at this

00:21:33.330 --> 00:21:35.390
run down here.

00:21:35.390 --> 00:21:42.330
I've done the same run except rather than
asking max_target_seqs [-] 1, I say max_target_seqs

00:21:42.330 --> 00:21:49.520
10, I want to see 10 matches, this gives me
more to look at.

00:21:49.520 --> 00:21:54.830
But maybe it's a little bit more revealing,
and it depends upon what I want, and I'm seeing

00:21:54.830 --> 00:22:03.300
my 10 matches, the interesting thing here,
is that, this is the max target sequence,

00:22:03.300 --> 00:22:11.760
and I actually have five matches with the
same score, they are all equivalent and really

00:22:11.760 --> 00:22:18.330
there is no way to say one is better than
the other as far as BLAST is concerned.

00:22:18.330 --> 00:22:24.410
These are all E. coli.

00:22:24.410 --> 00:22:28.710
Maybe that doesn't make any difference to
you, depending on your use case.

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:32.760
This is E.coli and I'm happy with that.

00:22:32.760 --> 00:22:38.320
there may be other examples where we could
find one of the E.coli strains that is pathogenic,

00:22:38.320 --> 00:22:44.110
or maybe it matches exactly with some other
bacteria.

00:22:44.110 --> 00:22:45.370
So that's a question.

00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:52.770
By looking at the first one, You are actually
throwing away some of this information.

00:22:52.770 --> 00:22:57.830
Here you see that there are basically different
strains are listed here.

00:22:57.830 --> 00:23:00.040
You've lost that information by only looking
at one.

00:23:00.040 --> 00:23:06.420
Of course, it depends on what you are looking
to do with this, whether you can be satisfied

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:11.240
with just one, or maybe you want to go for
a few and double check that.

00:23:11.240 --> 00:23:17.640
This is actually kind of the dangers of looking
at just one match.

00:23:17.640 --> 00:23:20.630
Now switching gears a little bit.

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:25.600
I want to talk about different types of reports
that you can produce.

00:23:25.600 --> 00:23:33.370
Since this talk is geared towards standalone
BLAST plus, I'm going to go through different

00:23:33.370 --> 00:23:37.760
reports that are easier to parse and that
people might find useful when they are running

00:23:37.760 --> 00:23:38.760
BLAST.

00:23:38.760 --> 00:23:44.140
Especially as part of a pipeline.

00:23:44.140 --> 00:23:46.490
The first one here is a standard BLAST report.

00:23:46.490 --> 00:23:49.640
It's been around for a long time.

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:55.570
This actually is the command line that produces
this BLAST report, you see the blastn, the

00:23:55.570 --> 00:24:02.520
database is refseq_rna, the query is U0001,
I put it in fasta here, and I'm just taking

00:24:02.520 --> 00:24:04.000
the first 40 lines here.

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:08.263
If you are familiar with unix or linux, 'head'
just means give me the first 40 lines [note:

00:24:08.263 --> 00:24:12.190
10 is default, -# is a specific number of
lines], I'm taking the first 40 lines here.

00:24:12.190 --> 00:24:15.870
We see a reference [to blast related publication],
we see the name of the database, the query

00:24:15.870 --> 00:24:21.410
and what the query is, the length of the query
and these different matches that are produced,

00:24:21.410 --> 00:24:27.410
significant alignments, statistically significant,
the bit score is over here.

00:24:27.410 --> 00:24:31.480
And there you see the E value, and you notice
that all these E values that are coming up

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:38.160
are zero, just because there are a lot of
different copies of this mRNA in refseq_rna

00:24:38.160 --> 00:24:40.550
database.

00:24:40.550 --> 00:24:45.390
Even though this is running in megablast node
here, there is absolutely no problem of finding

00:24:45.390 --> 00:24:48.640
lots of results.

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:56.110
This report is designed to be human readable,
it is subject to change.

00:24:56.110 --> 00:25:01.180
And we do not encourage you to parse it, and
that's actually why we produce some other

00:25:01.180 --> 00:25:07.770
formats that are easier to parse and provide
better support for that.

00:25:07.770 --> 00:25:14.720
One that people actually like a lot is the
so-called tabular report, here is a command

00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:18.620
line, which shows you how to run this.

00:25:18.620 --> 00:25:28.710
To produce this output, we use -outfmt 7,
it's going to give me the default tabular

00:25:28.710 --> 00:25:35.710
report, which is the query and subject, and
the percent identity, and some other information

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:37.790
you can look at.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:43.470
The main thing being, here you have the expect
value which you saw on the normal blast report,

00:25:43.470 --> 00:25:49.200
and here you have the bit scores, and it gives
you the start and stop of the matches, and

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:54.440
so if you could go through and parse this
and say, Oh, well, my query matched these

00:25:54.440 --> 00:25:58.660
database sequences starting here to there.

00:25:58.660 --> 00:26:04.180
Maybe I will pick this out and go look at
it some more.

00:26:04.180 --> 00:26:06.660
This is simple to parse, it is nice.

00:26:06.660 --> 00:26:14.280
Now if this is not all the information people
ever want, out of BLAST, that's why we have

00:26:14.280 --> 00:26:17.700
a customized tabular report.

00:26:17.700 --> 00:26:25.730
To do that it's -outfmt seven and quer accession
[qaccver], subject accession [saccver], length,

00:26:25.730 --> 00:26:30.240
the length of the alignment, and this is the
subject length.

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:35.481
This is giving you pretty basic information,
it is not giving you the expect value, it's

00:26:35.481 --> 00:26:42.730
not giving you the bit score, so you see here
this is quer all the time, these are the different

00:26:42.730 --> 00:26:45.870
database sequences.

00:26:45.870 --> 00:26:50.080
These are the lengths of the database sequences.

00:26:50.080 --> 00:26:53.660
I'm sorry, these are the lengths of the alignment
[third column] and then these are the lengths

00:26:53.660 --> 00:26:55.690
of the database sequences.

00:26:55.690 --> 00:27:03.830
For example, you can write an auk script that
will parse this and you can take and divide

00:27:03.830 --> 00:27:10.530
this length by that length, to tell you how
much of your subject sequence was covered

00:27:10.530 --> 00:27:11.940
by your query.

00:27:11.940 --> 00:27:17.010
Or how much of your subject sequences was
actually covered by the alignment, maybe you

00:27:17.010 --> 00:27:21.690
want matches that cover most of the subject
sequence.

00:27:21.690 --> 00:27:25.480
Something else you can do is to go for taxonomy.

00:27:25.480 --> 00:27:27.540
So this is the same thing here.

00:27:27.540 --> 00:27:35.040
Query accession, subject accession, the scientific
name, and then the common name and that's

00:27:35.040 --> 00:27:41.780
for the subject sequence in both cases, and
so you see here this is Homo sapiens, human,

00:27:41.780 --> 00:27:50.950
this is pygmy chimpanzee, and this gives you
taxonomy information without telling you,

00:27:50.950 --> 00:27:55.540
you're not looking at expect value or bit
score here, but you could print those out

00:27:55.540 --> 00:27:58.700
here but I didn't bother to keep it simple.

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:02.710
This gives you specialized taxonomy information.

00:28:02.710 --> 00:28:12.870
For the same thing, CSV, comma separated value,
you should be able to upload it, pull it into

00:28:12.870 --> 00:28:16.880
Excel or parse it in some other way.

00:28:16.880 --> 00:28:19.130
That gives you a lot of flexibility.

00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:28.841
Something else that people have liked, has
been the JSON, I think of this as a blast

00:28:28.841 --> 00:28:32.030
report in JSON.

00:28:32.030 --> 00:28:37.970
And here for example you see the reference
up here, see some of the parameter information,

00:28:37.970 --> 00:28:45.220
the query ID, if you go down here, you're
seeing the information about your first match.

00:28:45.220 --> 00:28:48.670
You have the ID, the accession.

00:28:48.670 --> 00:28:57.401
Nine 606 means it's human, so the taxid, the
NCBI Taxonomy group assigns a taxid to every

00:28:57.401 --> 00:29:00.960
node in the taxonomy tree, and for human it
is 9606.

00:29:00.960 --> 00:29:07.950
That's just a convenient way of referring,
at least in programs of referring back to

00:29:07.950 --> 00:29:11.480
a specific node in a taxonomy tree.

00:29:11.480 --> 00:29:21.080
This is actually giving you a lot of information
here in an easily parseable way, in JSON from

00:29:21.080 --> 00:29:26.260
a Python script, load it in, and parse this
out pretty quickly.

00:29:26.260 --> 00:29:31.290
Basically we have XML format that is the same
thing.

00:29:31.290 --> 00:29:38.140
One comment, you may know we are the NCBI,
is moving away from gi's and towards accessions,

00:29:38.140 --> 00:29:44.390
I suggest that you stick with the accessions
here rather than being stuck in the gi's that

00:29:44.390 --> 00:29:46.050
may disappear from you.

00:29:46.050 --> 00:29:56.400
Oh, sorry, this is the outfmt 15 and the command
line here.

00:29:56.400 --> 00:29:59.710
I showed you a bunch of different reports.

00:29:59.710 --> 00:30:01.640
You could imagine they have different uses.

00:30:01.640 --> 00:30:09.070
For example, I run blast, I want to get a
tabular report, and I only want to look at

00:30:09.070 --> 00:30:17.120
matches that cover my query, only have a certain
coverage of my query, or maybe have a certain

00:30:17.120 --> 00:30:18.380
taxonomy.

00:30:18.380 --> 00:30:21.940
Or a certain bit score.

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:27.790
If they reach some threshold, maybe I want
to go back and look at this manually, otherwise

00:30:27.790 --> 00:30:31.750
I will just discard this.

00:30:31.750 --> 00:30:39.020
So, this would almost indicate that you need
to run BLAST multiple times, but that is actually

00:30:39.020 --> 00:30:40.340
not the case.

00:30:40.340 --> 00:30:45.280
Can BLAST has something that you can run it
once, and then you can enjoy your results,

00:30:45.280 --> 00:30:49.570
so to speak, many times with the blast_formatter.

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:54.071
This is an example, this is the command line
I would run for blastn, and this is the database

00:30:54.071 --> 00:31:02.310
refseq_rna, and the query, output format 11,
and it produces BLAST archived format.

00:31:02.310 --> 00:31:09.150
The blast_formatter is a separate program,
can read in this file here, and it can format

00:31:09.150 --> 00:31:18.120
it as requested, for example, this is outfmt
7, the standard tabular format, basically

00:31:18.120 --> 00:31:20.460
all of these examples I showed you earlier.

00:31:20.460 --> 00:31:26.840
So you could, run this one right here, and
if you decided that the length of the alignment

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:31.530
was long enough, and overcame some threshold,
and thought that was interesting to look at,

00:31:31.530 --> 00:31:38.510
you could go down and look and spit out a
BLAST report.

00:31:38.510 --> 00:31:40.510
And that gives you a lot of flexibility.

00:31:40.510 --> 00:31:42.980
Shifting gear again.

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:48.780
Hopefully I'm not doing too bad on time, and
I want to talk about taxonomic limits, it

00:31:48.780 --> 00:31:54.130
can clear the clutter, meaning I got a lot
of results and I don't want to see them all,

00:31:54.130 --> 00:31:59.660
maybe I only want to see the human results
or maybe I don't want to see the human results,

00:31:59.660 --> 00:32:04.340
maybe I only want to see bacterial results,
and it runs faster.

00:32:04.340 --> 00:32:09.420
In general at least, because you have less
of a database to search.

00:32:09.420 --> 00:32:18.250
Here is an example, I've got RefSeq protein,
this human protein which I am searching against

00:32:18.250 --> 00:32:21.270
refseq_protein, limited to human.

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:26.810
I see then all of these, well first, all these
matches say human here.

00:32:26.810 --> 00:32:29.650
You will see I have different components,
different isoforms.

00:32:29.650 --> 00:32:34.920
I have a lot of variety.

00:32:34.920 --> 00:32:36.450
These two proteins are related.

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:40.400
But I'm getting all the different isoforms.

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:44.950
This is all the results I get, I get 12 results.

00:32:44.950 --> 00:32:49.120
That is actually great, because I can actually
look through those if I'm interested in different

00:32:49.120 --> 00:32:52.560
isoforms, then now I have that information.

00:32:52.560 --> 00:32:55.830
If I do the same thing without a limit.

00:32:55.830 --> 00:33:02.030
You know first of all it's 507 hits, you're
not seeing all the hits here.

00:33:02.030 --> 00:33:05.510
I'm seeing it from different organisms.

00:33:05.510 --> 00:33:09.540
From gorilla and chimp and everything else.

00:33:09.540 --> 00:33:18.180
What is different here also is I'm really
only seem to be seeing one version of the

00:33:18.180 --> 00:33:19.180
protein.

00:33:19.180 --> 00:33:20.180
One isoform here.

00:33:20.180 --> 00:33:23.740
They all say component A2.

00:33:23.740 --> 00:33:25.540
This is actually a different use case.

00:33:25.540 --> 00:33:31.450
I've got one protein, one isoform, I want
to see how it appears in all kinds of different

00:33:31.450 --> 00:33:33.460
organisms.

00:33:33.460 --> 00:33:39.130
Maybe I want to make a tree with this, maybe
I want to find out, if this isoform which

00:33:39.130 --> 00:33:40.780
organisms it appears in.

00:33:40.780 --> 00:33:44.530
That is a different use case.

00:33:44.530 --> 00:33:54.040
Recently we have made enhancements to help
people with this search by taxonomy, we are

00:33:54.040 --> 00:34:00.930
calling this BLASTDBv5, I apologize for the
programmer speak, and the reason why it's

00:34:00.930 --> 00:34:05.930
called version 5 is the current version of
blast database [BLASTDB] is version 4, but

00:34:05.930 --> 00:34:11.000
what it lets you do is limit your search by
taxonomy with a command line parameter, and

00:34:11.000 --> 00:34:16.001
the taxonomy information is built into the
database in a way that we can use it to limit

00:34:16.001 --> 00:34:19.440
your search.

00:34:19.440 --> 00:34:25.000
The other thing about BLASTDBv5 is it improves
your performance when limiting a blast search

00:34:25.000 --> 00:34:30.329
with accessions or retrieving by accession
and this is important as the NCBI moves from

00:34:30.329 --> 00:34:34.579
gi's which are numbers to accessions.

00:34:34.579 --> 00:34:38.639
You can also retrieve sequences by taxonomy
from the blast database, and it works with

00:34:38.639 --> 00:34:44.399
the newest version of BLAST plus which was
made in an alpha release several months but

00:34:44.399 --> 00:34:50.879
we're going to have an upcoming release in
hopefully this month.

00:34:50.879 --> 00:34:59.940
Peter did a webinar on this, several months
ago, there is a YouTube link here and documentation

00:34:59.940 --> 00:35:01.070
that you can get to.

00:35:01.070 --> 00:35:05.220
Since these slides are available, you can
get to those.

00:35:05.220 --> 00:35:09.349
So moving on here.

00:35:09.349 --> 00:35:17.819
I will tell you a little bit about Magic-BLAST,
it's a splice aware aligner, it's based on

00:35:17.819 --> 00:35:25.359
the aligner in the magic pipeline that Jean
and Danielle Thierry-Mieg had made and we

00:35:25.359 --> 00:35:28.210
re-implemented that with a BLAST toolkit.

00:35:28.210 --> 00:35:31.880
It uses blast databases to do its mapping.

00:35:31.880 --> 00:35:40.609
It's especially good with longer sequences,
and we have an article actually, we have a

00:35:40.609 --> 00:35:47.119
preprint in bio-archive [bioRxiv.org], that
we'll be submitting to a journal soon, so

00:35:47.119 --> 00:35:56.190
this is a figure from that preprint, and this
is a set we made up.

00:35:56.190 --> 00:36:03.849
The reads are based on the RefSeq annotation,
and so that we know what the results really

00:36:03.849 --> 00:36:05.170
should be like.

00:36:05.170 --> 00:36:09.660
Magic-BLAST was not used in the RefSeq annotation
so it is not a circular logic here.

00:36:09.660 --> 00:36:14.900
But you can see with Magic-BLAST for these
longer sequences here, we do pretty well,

00:36:14.900 --> 00:36:19.900
perfect matches is 97.2% and some partial
and some misaligned.

00:36:19.900 --> 00:36:26.589
We're comparing this to the HISAT2 with relaxed
parameters, HISAT2, STAR long and the older

00:36:26.589 --> 00:36:29.789
TopHat2.

00:36:29.789 --> 00:36:35.090
Real quick, because I think I've probably
run out of my time, you can see the URL at

00:36:35.090 --> 00:36:42.920
the top [of the slide], you can download standalone
BLAST down at the bottom there, and over here

00:36:42.920 --> 00:36:46.660
in the right corner, you can see where there
is help you can get.

00:36:46.660 --> 00:36:54.661
And we also have AMI, you know an Amazon Machine
Image, in an AWS image that you can run, and

00:36:54.661 --> 00:36:58.980
which you can get it from there.

00:36:58.980 --> 00:37:04.610
Resources, some of the resources for command
line options, and these are actually the appendices

00:37:04.610 --> 00:37:10.040
in the BLAST manual, and so this is several
tables of the command line options that you

00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:12.140
can go and look at them.

00:37:12.140 --> 00:37:20.089
There is another appendix that reward mismatch
penalties and that applies to blastn and megaBLAST,

00:37:20.089 --> 00:37:26.230
available scoring matrices, and this is more
BLASTp, blastX, and then there's a link to

00:37:26.230 --> 00:37:29.750
the Magic-BLAST article.

00:37:29.750 --> 00:37:38.150
After this is a few more slides I will not
go into, that's an appendix that I call the

00:37:38.150 --> 00:37:41.559
hidden life of the BLAST database, but we
didn't have time for that but if you're wanting

00:37:41.559 --> 00:37:46.119
to take a look at, then you're welcome to
do that.

00:37:46.119 --> 00:37:50.160
[Peter] This is Peter Cooper again, we have
a lot of questions.

00:37:50.160 --> 00:37:54.640
More than I've ever seen in a webinar before
to be hone with you, which is good.

00:37:54.640 --> 00:38:01.200
Many of these are detailed questions about
how do I do a particular task.

00:38:01.200 --> 00:38:05.819
I think I can't even answer those because
I would have to go and look at certain things

00:38:05.819 --> 00:38:10.190
to give you the correct, exact, Entrez queries
and things like that.

00:38:10.190 --> 00:38:14.440
For those things, I've answered some of them
in the chat [window], but I haven't been able

00:38:14.440 --> 00:38:15.440
to answer all of them.

00:38:15.440 --> 00:38:18.040
I will make a document with the answers to
those.

00:38:18.040 --> 00:38:21.730
I'll post that and pass it off to everybody.

00:38:21.730 --> 00:38:25.349
One thing that has come up several times,
because it was announced several times ahead

00:38:25.349 --> 00:38:33.420
of time, Tom might be able to discuss this
a little more was the max target sequence

00:38:33.420 --> 00:38:36.680
letter that was in Bioinformatics.

00:38:36.680 --> 00:38:39.839
So there has been a lot of confusion.

00:38:39.839 --> 00:38:45.589
I guess, there's several questions in chat
pod about that, and people want to know what

00:38:45.589 --> 00:38:50.140
is the effective max target sequence, and
what's the recommendation for how they should

00:38:50.140 --> 00:38:52.319
use that parameter?

00:38:52.319 --> 00:38:54.880
How does it affect what results they see back?

00:38:54.880 --> 00:39:00.210
In other words, it's clear from reading the
questions, that many people believe it will

00:39:00.210 --> 00:39:04.430
show you the first match it finds rather than
the best one, which I don't think is true.

00:39:04.430 --> 00:39:07.800
But I will let you address that, if you don't
mind.

00:39:07.800 --> 00:39:11.280
[Tom] So, thanks, Peter.

00:39:11.280 --> 00:39:17.950
So BLAST goes through the database, looking
for the best matches, collects those, and

00:39:17.950 --> 00:39:20.250
then presents them to you.

00:39:20.250 --> 00:39:25.450
I kind of, can I go back here.

00:39:25.450 --> 00:39:32.599
This is starting to address here, 'Limiting
output to one match' [slide] so what BLAST

00:39:32.599 --> 00:39:38.710
does, it goes through your database, finds
the best matches, ranks them by score and

00:39:38.710 --> 00:39:45.400
expect value, in this case, in the bottom
set, there are five matches that are basically

00:39:45.400 --> 00:39:50.190
equivalent, and BLAST can't say that one is
better than the other.

00:39:50.190 --> 00:39:54.700
It does show you, the user, if you say you
only want to see one, it will show you the

00:39:54.700 --> 00:39:57.619
top one.

00:39:57.619 --> 00:40:02.420
Even if you say, I only want to see one, blast
is still going to go through the entire database,

00:40:02.420 --> 00:40:05.450
it's still going to collect these five matches.

00:40:05.450 --> 00:40:10.760
It actually collects a few more matches, actually,
many more matches than it presents to you

00:40:10.760 --> 00:40:15.880
because, and this is just part of the algorithm
to make sure that we don't miss stuff.

00:40:15.880 --> 00:40:20.109
Or to make it very unlikely that we miss stuff.

00:40:20.109 --> 00:40:26.509
There had been the question, max target sequences
is 1, when I think about it, generally I would

00:40:26.509 --> 00:40:31.849
recommend that you probably want to look at
more than one sequence, again it depends on

00:40:31.849 --> 00:40:38.329
your use case, in this case I have 10, and
I saw a bunch that were the same.

00:40:38.329 --> 00:40:42.549
Maybe if you don't want to look at a lot,
then 10 is a good number.

00:40:42.549 --> 00:40:49.881
I would probably shy away from one unless
you have a case like, any hit at all, that

00:40:49.881 --> 00:40:55.049
you are only interested if there is any match
at all, and so something that comes to my

00:40:55.049 --> 00:41:02.450
mind, maybe people have other examples, would
be say I done some bacterial sequencing, I

00:41:02.450 --> 00:41:06.650
know the results should be bacterial, but
they've been done by humans, and I want to

00:41:06.650 --> 00:41:10.882
make sure that there is no human DNA involved
in there, that I didn't contaminate it somehow

00:41:10.882 --> 00:41:16.970
with my own DNA, I take my sequences and I
run these sequences against the human database,

00:41:16.970 --> 00:41:22.170
and any match is going to be disqualifying
for this read, for this query and I will just

00:41:22.170 --> 00:41:24.470
throw it away because it is contamination.

00:41:24.470 --> 00:41:30.141
That's an example I think maybe where max
target sequence is one would be okay, but

00:41:30.141 --> 00:41:37.220
generally you want to go through several,
and like Peter said, there is some confusion

00:41:37.220 --> 00:41:42.630
about how BLAST goes through the database.

00:41:42.630 --> 00:41:48.359
It does go through all the database, collects
all matches, and there is an example that

00:41:48.359 --> 00:41:55.810
you could, and in this letter that he mentioned,
there wasn't the example that I really wanted

00:41:55.810 --> 00:41:56.810
to see.

00:41:56.810 --> 00:42:02.220
I kind of need to see the command line parameters
and such, you can imagine, you take all these

00:42:02.220 --> 00:42:09.019
sequences, I reorder them and maybe BLAST
will give me a different top match but maybe

00:42:09.019 --> 00:42:13.599
if I were to take my database and reorder
it, maybe I would get the second match here

00:42:13.599 --> 00:42:15.119
instead of the first one.

00:42:15.119 --> 00:42:22.359
It is the same significance.

00:42:22.359 --> 00:42:27.440
Blast does have some tie-breaking logic in
it, to help people, that is getting into the

00:42:27.440 --> 00:42:34.190
technical details again, basically my point
of view when I think about it and I hadn't

00:42:34.190 --> 00:42:40.079
thought about it until the last week, is max_target_seqs
equal 1 is not really a great choice.

